Hi Jeffrey Doser,

I am fitting multi-species occupancy models in spOccupancy with arboreal camera-trap data from the Magdalena Medio region, Colombia. The data are 51 arboreal camera stations (deployed at staggered dates, 83–160 days each) and 4 primate species. Naive occupancy (cameras with detections) is 42/51 for Cebus versicolor, 33/51 for Aotus griseimembra, 15/51 for Alouatta seniculus and 10/51 for Ateles hybridus. I am thinking to use 14-day occasions counted from each camera's own deployment date.
I have two questions:

1) With only N = 4 species, would you recommend spMsPGOcc (one spatial process per species) or sfMsPGOcc with a single factor (q = 1) to account for residual species correlations? I read in Doser et al. (2023) that the factor approach is designed for larger communities, so I am unsure whether it is advisable here. But, in primates in really fragemented areas species correlations can happen.

2) Is there any guidance on how many occurrence covariates are reasonable with 51 sites. I want to include some environmental covariates and some climate covariates. But askingt to IA,it recommends me máximun 4, in particular when one species (Ateles) was detected at only 10 stations.

Thank you very much for your time and for the package.

Best regards,
Karol Aguilar

